>>
endobj
354 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 121 0 R
/E (and the algorithm scales poorly as n increases. The final n = 7,500 experiment provides a direct empirical confirmation because 28,121,250 pair computations are recorded. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [19]
>>
endobj
355 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 121 0 R
/E (The Divide-and-Conquer method follows the classical closest-pair strategy. The points are organized by x-coordinate and y-coordinate, the set is divided around the median x- coordinate, each half is solved recursively, and a strip around the dividing line is checked for cross-boundary pairs that could improve the current minimum. The recursion reduces the active problem size while the strip-processing step prevents an exhaustive cross-comparison of the two halves. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [20]
>>
endobj
356 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 121 0 R
/E (The Hybrid algorithm is the principal development in this case study. CO2 contributes the direct Brute-Force leaf computation, CO3 contributes the recursive partitioning framework, and CO1 provides the complexity-analysis and experimental-method framework used to determine when each method is preferable. The resulting design preserves the asymptotic structure of Divide and Conquer while changing the constant-factor behavior at small recursive subproblems. This is the key distinction between simply implementing three algorithms and developing an integrated algorithmic solution. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [21]
>>
endobj
